// =====================================================================
// D0053 (PROJECT numbering) — STPA becomes an activated project PROCESS with ordered steps, and every hazard-bearing
// state in the client is a machine on one shared primitive, registered on its STPA process model. PROPOSED, not yet
// accepted: this adds a process definition and a deploying skill (§3a / §4; the D0070 process-change keystone
// requires a #ProspectiveChange Decision with a human signature). The AI applied the change in the same commit in
// the D0049 shape so the gate ran on it; the owner accepts or rejects via `keel accept d0053`.
// =====================================================================
package ProjectDecision0053 {
    private import EngineElement::*;
    private import EngineWork::*;
    private import EngineVerification::*;
    private import EngineRelationships::*;

    #ProspectiveChange part d0053 : Decision {
        :>> id = "237e6e94-ce84-4641-9c76-5f7e1ab90628";
        :>> title = "D0053: STPA is an activated project process (Steps 1–6) deployed by the safety-analysis skill, and hazard-bearing state is a fsm.ts machine registered on its STPA process model";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-08";
        :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus";
        :>> status = DecisionStatus::proposed;
        :>> context = "Owner, 2026-09-08, after a conflict-resolution flow was rebuilt as a state machine: 'FSM module, used all across the project in places where states that can introduce hazards exist. do we have an activated STPA process with step 1, step 2, etc.?' The facts: STPA ran here as a SKILL (.engine/skills/stpa + references/sop.md, D0016) — the analysis itself is complete in .tracking/safety (5 losses, 10 hazards, 7 controllers, 22 UCAs, 19 scenarios, 25 SRs, every SR #Verify-linked as of e540b786) — but there was no `stpa` PROCESS: no ProcessSteps, no successions, no APPLIES-WHEN, no reanalysis trigger, and `keel activation` listed only the engine's stpa-diagram. Separately the client held five hand-rolled state machines (connstate linkNext, syncengine EngineState, mountfsm, transportstate, and the new resolvefsm), each a pure reducer of its own shape, none built on a shared primitive, none named by the STPA controller or process model it realizes — so 'a state that can introduce a hazard' was a switch statement the safety analysis could not see. The 2026-09-07 incident (a Keep pressed while a 152 s pass was running over the same files, S-20) is exactly a resolver whose process model had no belief about the engine's state.";
        :>> decision = "(1) .engine/processes/stpa.sysml: a project Process with six ordered steps — 1 purpose (losses, hazards, constraints), 2 control structure, 3 UCAs by the four guide words, 4 loss scenarios + verified safety requirements, 5 reanalysis-on-change (D0017's trigger; automatic propagation stays upstream, sysmlv2-ai-toolkit #66), 6 hazard-bearing state is a registered state machine — with `first…then` successions and an APPLIES-WHEN. Deployed (D0059) by a new project skill .engine/skills/safety-analysis, which wraps the engine's stpa SOP for Steps 1–4 and carries Steps 5–6; the engine's own stpa skill is untouched (engine-owned). (2) client/src/fsm.ts is THE state-machine primitive: defineMachine(spec) — pure (state, event) -> state [+ effects as data], total and conservative (no rule => unchanged, never throws), declared terminal states with explicit exits, a tableCoverage test helper — and every machine is NAMED after the STPA process model it realizes. (3) Registration is in the model: a `// STPA-FSM: <module>#<machine>` line inside the ProcessModel it realizes (control-structure.sysml); a machine that cannot yet move is `// STPA-FSM-PENDING: <module> (<issue>)` with a tracked resolver. client/test/fsm-conformance.test.ts enforces both directions on every run: every registered machine exists, imports fsm.ts and names its process model; every module that defines a machine is registered; a PENDING module is not yet on the primitive. (4) Applied now: resolvefsm (pmResolver), mountfsm + transportstate (pmSyncEngine), connstate linkMachine (pmSession) are on the primitive with their exported signatures unchanged; syncengine's EngineState is PENDING (issueEngineFsmNotOnPrimitive, engineFsmOnPrimitive). (5) The conflict resolver joins the control structure as c8ConflictResolver with pmResolver, three control actions, fbEngineBusy, UCA-23..25, C-21..23, S-20, SR-26/27 — the Step 3/4 pass this decision's own trigger demands.";
        :>> rationale = "A safety analysis is only as good as its visibility into the states that can go wrong. With five reducers of five shapes and no registration, Step 3 could not say which control actions a state transition issues, Step 5 had nothing to hang a reanalysis on, and a new machine (resolvefsm, this week) could be added with no safety question asked at all. One primitive gives every machine the same three properties the analysis needs — a total table (tableCoverage proves nothing throws and nothing invents a state), declared terminal states (the irreversible ones), effects as data (what a transition DOES is enumerable) — and the registration makes the mapping machine <-> controller a fact in the model that a test enforces, not a comment. Making STPA a process rather than a skill matters for the same reason the engine makes everything else a process (D0059): a skill is applied by vigilance, a process has steps a ceremony can point at, an APPLIES-WHEN onboarding can recommend, and a Step 5 trigger that names when the analysis is stale. ALTERNATIVES: (i) leave STPA as a skill and the FSMs as they were — REJECTED, it is exactly the state the owner objected to; (ii) a heavyweight FSM library — REJECTED, the five machines are small pure reducers and the property wanted is uniformity + enumerability, not hierarchical states; ~80 lines of primitive give that; (iii) refactor syncengine now too — REJECTED for this commit: its transitions are interleaved with effects in a class and deserve a behaviour-preserving sprint of their own with a Step 5 reanalysis of c2SyncEngine, so it is registered PENDING with a tracked resolver rather than half-moved. WHY PROPOSED: adding a process definition and a deploying skill is a process/schema-surface change (§3a, §4, D0070); the owner asked the question, the answer is a change to how the project works, and that carries a human signature.";
        :>> consequences = "`keel activation` lists stpa as an always-on project process; process-skill and process-applicability guards pass (safety-analysis names the process file; APPLIES-WHEN present). fsm.test + fsm-conformance.test run in the client suite (912 -> 924 tests at introduction). control-structure.sysml carries the STPA-FSM registrations; the safety model gains c8ConflictResolver (+ pmResolver, 3 control actions, 1 feedback, 3 UCAs, 3 constraints, 1 scenario, 2 SRs, all typed, SR-26/27 #Verify-linked to passed DoDs). Open follow-up: engineFsmOnPrimitive (syncengine on the primitive + the PENDING flip). REMOVAL PATH: `git revert` of the commit restores the skill-only STPA and the hand-rolled reducers; the safety-model additions are plain items and edges and would revert with it. Accept with `keel accept d0053 --by wweatherholtz --note ... --date ...` from the owner's own terminal.";
    }

    verification d0053Accept : Test {
        :>> id = "580ee957-98fc-46a5-adee-4d7ddf5012eb";
        :>> method = VerificationMethod::confirmation;
        :>> procedureText = "The owner accepts STPA as an activated project process (Steps 1-6, safety-analysis skill) and fsm.ts as the mandated primitive for hazard-bearing state, registered on STPA process models. HUMAN attestation via `keel accept d0053`; never inferred from the directive that prompted the work.";
    }
}
